---
import { Code } from "astro:components";
import sqlFeatureMatrix from "@minnowdb/core/sql-feature-matrix.json";
import Docs from "../../layouts/Docs.astro";

interface SqlFeature {
  id: string;
  status: "supported" | "unsupported";
  example: string;
  /** Bound values for the example's placeholders, in order. */
  params?: Array<string | number | boolean | null>;
  error?: string;
  notes?: string;
}

function exampleWithParams(feature: SqlFeature): string {
  return feature.params === undefined
    ? feature.example
    : `${feature.example}\n-- bound: ${JSON.stringify(feature.params)}`;
}

const features = (sqlFeatureMatrix as { features: SqlFeature[] }).features;
const supported = features.filter((feature) => feature.status === "supported");
const unsupported = features.filter((feature) => feature.status !== "supported");
const themes = { light: "github-light", dark: "github-dark" } as const;
---

<Docs
  title="SQL support"
  description="The checked-in SQL feature matrix: every supported and rejected form, with examples."
>
  <h1>SQL support</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    The SQL surface is recorded in a checked-in feature matrix that ships with the package
    (<code>@minnowdb/core/sql-feature-matrix.json</code>). The engine's conformance suite reports
    drift against this file, so this page and the engine cannot silently disagree. Beyond the
    matrix, a differential harness executes a generated query corpus through the engine's two
    executors and through two independent oracles — native SQLite and PGlite — on every test
    run; where the surfaces overlap, the results must agree.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>{supported.length}</strong> of <strong>{features.length}</strong> cataloged SQL
    features are supported; <strong>{unsupported.length}</strong> are explicitly not.
  </p>

  <h2>Supported</h2>
  <div class="feature-list">
    {
      supported.map((feature) => (
        <article class="sql-feature supported">
          <div class="feature-head">
            <code class="feature-id">{feature.id}</code>
            <span class="badge yes">supported</span>
          </div>
          <Code code={exampleWithParams(feature)} lang="sql" themes={themes} defaultColor={false} />
          {feature.notes !== undefined && <small>{feature.notes}</small>}
        </article>
      ))
    }
  </div>

  <h2>Explicitly unsupported</h2>
  <p>
    These forms are rejected with a typed error rather than silently misbehaving. A note
    distinguishes a missing capability from one reachable through a typed API, or from a deliberate
    restriction.
  </p>
  <div class="feature-list">
    {
      unsupported.map((feature) => (
        <article class="sql-feature unsupported">
          <div class="feature-head">
            <code class="feature-id">{feature.id}</code>
            <span class="badge no">not supported</span>
          </div>
          <Code code={exampleWithParams(feature)} lang="sql" themes={themes} defaultColor={false} />
          {feature.error !== undefined && <em class="feature-error">{feature.error}</em>}
          {feature.notes !== undefined && <small>{feature.notes}</small>}
        </article>
      ))
    }
  </div>

  <h2 id="deliberate-omissions">Deliberate omissions</h2>
  <p>
    Minnow targets SQL:2016 wherever the standard makes sense inside a browser worker. The
    features below are omitted on purpose — each is a consequence of the runtime or the engine's
    design, not an oversight. Everything here fails with an explicit error if you write it.
  </p>

  <h3>Because the browser is the runtime</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Access control</strong> (roles, GRANT/REVOKE, row-level security) — a Minnow
      database lives inside one origin's storage and is reachable only by that origin's code. The
      browser sandbox is the security boundary; SQL-level identity would be theater.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Sessions, connections, and cursors</strong> — there is one engine in one worker.
      Queries return complete results over immutable snapshots; there is no connection to hold a
      cursor open on.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Isolation levels</strong> (SET TRANSACTION ISOLATION LEVEL) — every read is
      snapshot-isolated and writes serialize through MVCC commit. There is no weaker level to
      opt into, so the knob does not exist.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Session time zones</strong> (SET TIME ZONE, AT TIME ZONE) — datetimes are stored
      and computed as UTC instants. A session time zone would make the same query return
      different rows on different machines, which an offline-capable local database cannot
      tolerate. Convert for display in your UI layer.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Locale collation</strong> (COLLATE) — strings compare and sort in codepoint order,
      identically in every browser. Locale-aware collation would bundle ICU tables or depend on
      host-locale behavior that differs across devices; use the client layer for display sorting.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Stored procedures, SQL/PSM, and user-defined functions</strong> — the language for
      extending Minnow is JavaScript, in the same process, with types. Registering JS functions
      into SQL would make query results depend on code the engine cannot snapshot or replay.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>SQL-level BEGIN/COMMIT/SAVEPOINT</strong> — an atomic multi-statement scope is the
      engine's programmatic API (<code>database.write(async (tx) =&gt; …)</code>), and each SQL
      statement outside one is atomic on its own. Holding an open interactive transaction across
      await points in a browser tab (which can be frozen or killed at any moment) invites lost
      work; the engine's write path is designed around that reality.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Engine-design decisions</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Keyless UPDATE/DELETE</strong> — mutation segments address rows by unique key;
      tables without one are append-only by construction.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Correlated NOT IN and non-equality correlation</strong> — correlated subqueries
      decorrelate into joins at compile time, which requires equality keys; NOT IN's NULL
      semantics cannot survive that rewrite. NOT EXISTS expresses the same intent.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>GROUPING()</strong> — ROLLUP/CUBE/GROUPING SETS desugar into unions, so the
      grouping marker has nothing to read; rollup NULLs and data NULLs are indistinguishable.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Not yet, rather than never</h3>
  <p>
    MERGE (ON CONFLICT covers upserting), LATERAL, SQL/JSON functions, and named WINDOW clauses
    have no browser-shaped reason to be missing — they are simply not built yet, and each rejects
    with an explicit error today.
  </p>
</Docs>

<style>
  .feature-list {
    display: grid;
    gap: 0.9rem;
    margin: 1.2rem 0 2rem;
  }

  .sql-feature {
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: 8px;
    padding: 0.8rem 1rem;
  }

  .feature-head {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: space-between;
    gap: 0.75rem;
    margin-bottom: 0.4rem;
  }

  .feature-id {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.85rem;
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  .badge {
    font-size: 0.72rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    border-radius: 999px;
    padding: 0.12rem 0.6rem;
    white-space: nowrap;
  }

  .badge.yes {
    color: #0f7b3f;
    background: rgba(68, 131, 97, 0.14);
  }

  .badge.no {
    color: var(--code-inline);
    background: rgba(212, 76, 71, 0.12);
  }

  .sql-feature :global(pre.astro-code) {
    margin: 0.4rem 0;
    padding: 0.6rem 0.8rem;
    font-size: 0.8rem;
  }

  .feature-error {
    display: block;
    color: var(--text-secondary);
    font-size: 0.85rem;
    margin-top: 0.3rem;
  }

  .sql-feature small {
    display: block;
    color: var(--text-faint);
    margin-top: 0.3rem;
  }
</style>
